===== pm --help
usage: pm [-h]
          {show,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,where,clean,interactive,version,commit} ...

pm: the write path for project records, and project actions that touch both records and the work store.

positional arguments:
  {show,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,where,clean,interactive,version,commit}
    show                compact status of open projects for agents, level by level
    finding             sprint findings
    feedback            feedback on pm itself
    decision            decisions: record one, or ask the owner for one
    action              actions: ask the owner to do something (run, apply), or to review a PR (--pr)
    need                needs of any kind: decisions, actions and reviews
    reply               the owner's replies from the site
    doc                 free-form dated docs
    design              design pages: create or rename one, or record why one stays as it is
    postmortem          incident postmortems
    project             projects
    sprint              sprints
    task                tasks inside open sprints
    dep                 dependencies: what an item waits on
    comment             notes on items
    record              records on the served site
    check               check that every record renders with the work store, writing nothing; the check before a commit, and the one pm commit runs
    service             the pm service: one background process per clone serves the site, syncs the work store and pushes the records branch every 10 minutes
    doctor              compare every managed piece with what this pm writes, and the clone and worktree with what pm init makes; report each difference, exit 1 on any (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the work store cannot be checked: its line names the mismatch and the fix); fail when the CLI that summary_runtime in .pm/config.toml names (claude or codex) is not on PATH, since the pm service summarizes with it, and never for the other runtime, whose sessions, and so whose owner-request judge, run only where it is installed; on Linux, where Codex is installed, fail when its sandbox cannot start (codex sandbox -- true: bwrap cannot make its namespaces, as under Ubuntu 24.04's AppArmor), naming the cause and the fix, which pm doctor leaves to the user; and name a pm release newer than the pin, with its notes (or say the release list cannot be read), and the launcher's pins that no checkout on this machine pins, which pm upgrade prunes: both leave the exit code alone
    upgrade             first print the upgrade guide of each release the move crosses, after the pin up to this pm, oldest first, from the release notes this pm was built with (no network; it fails, writing nothing, when a crossed release's guide is missing); then move the pin in .pm/config.toml to this pm and rewrite every managed piece as it writes them (the fix pm doctor names for a changed one), removing the pre-package harness's and the ones an earlier pm wrote and this one retired (the records/ copy and guard workflows, the pre-commit section); writes files and prints the commit to make, never commits; then lists the clone's other worktrees whose checked-out branch pins another pm, where pm refuses every work-store command once the main checkout pins this one, each with 'merge or rebase it onto the pin move'; last, prune the launcher's pins (<data dir>/pm/pins/<X>/pm) that no checkout on this machine pins (each worktree of this clone and of every clone with a pm service unit), keeping each pin's sha256 so a later download is checked against it, and pruning none when any of those cannot be read
    uninstall           remove pm's pieces from this worktree (hook entries, .gitignore block, .pm/, an earlier pm's workflows and git hook sections in .pm/hooks) and the clone's and machine's setup (the store checkout, records/ links, records workspaces and their drafts/ branches and an earlier pm's sparse checkouts in every worktree, refused while a workspace has uncommitted edits; the pm service, this clone's Codex writable_roots, pm's lines in .git/info/exclude, a core.hooksPath an earlier pm pointed at .pm/hooks, the work store (refused while it holds what the remote lacks; with the pm service stopped, it runs pm service run for that check)); keeps the records branch and the remote's work store (refs/pm/work); never commits
    init                install pm: the repo's files on first install (--site-url sets the public site link, --listen and --access who reaches it), then this clone, this worktree and the pm service (PORT=<n> sets its port, and on a first install the config's; PORT=<n> pm service install moves only the service's); session start runs it; never commits on the code branch
    where               list every location with its state: the store, this checkout and its records/ (the main checkout's link to the store, or a worktree's records workspace: its branch, uncommitted files, conflict markers and how far it is behind the records branch), the work store (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the mismatch and the fix: merge or rebase onto main's pin move, or pm service restart), the Codex sandbox roots, the pm service, the last push, and the site; 'pm where records' prints only the store's path
    clean               list each worktree with keep or remove and the reason; --apply removes the agent worktrees (.claude/worktrees/) that no live session owns and whose work is saved, and the interactive-mode files of sessions idle for a day
    interactive         turn the owner-request Stop check off for this session, so asks in chat pass, until pm autopilot, and print the interactive request rule; run it only when the owner asks
    version             print this pm's version, dev for an untagged build; outside any repo too
    commit              commit your hand edits to records, named by path, on the records branch

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit

pm runs the pm version the repo pins in .pm/config.toml: another version's release binary, downloaded once; a pin below 0.2.0 (a retired Python release) is refused. pm where names the version running and why.

pm --help --all also lists the machinery commands: what the runtimes' hooks, the scheduler and the pm service run, not agents.
===== pm --help --all
usage: pm [-h]
          {show,day,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,sync,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,push,where,clean,interactive,autopilot,export,version,prime,hook,commit} ...

pm: the write path for project records, and project actions that touch both records and the work store.

positional arguments:
  {show,day,finding,feedback,decision,action,need,reply,doc,design,postmortem,project,sprint,task,dep,comment,record,check,sync,service,doctor,upgrade,uninstall,init,push,where,clean,interactive,autopilot,export,version,prime,hook,commit}
    show                compact status of open projects for agents, level by level
    day                 day pages: generated from the day's activity; nobody writes a day record
    finding             sprint findings
    feedback            feedback on pm itself
    decision            decisions: record one, or ask the owner for one
    action              actions: ask the owner to do something (run, apply), or to review a PR (--pr)
    need                needs of any kind: decisions, actions and reviews
    reply               the owner's replies from the site
    doc                 free-form dated docs
    design              design pages: create or rename one, or record why one stays as it is
    postmortem          incident postmortems
    project             projects
    sprint              sprints
    task                tasks inside open sprints
    dep                 dependencies: what an item waits on
    comment             notes on items
    record              records on the served site
    check               check that every record renders with the work store, writing nothing; the check before a commit, and the one pm commit runs
    sync                sync the work store with the remote now, as the pm service does every 10 minutes
    service             the pm service: one background process per clone serves the site, syncs the work store and pushes the records branch every 10 minutes
    doctor              compare every managed piece with what this pm writes, and the clone and worktree with what pm init makes; report each difference, exit 1 on any (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the work store cannot be checked: its line names the mismatch and the fix); fail when the CLI that summary_runtime in .pm/config.toml names (claude or codex) is not on PATH, since the pm service summarizes with it, and never for the other runtime, whose sessions, and so whose owner-request judge, run only where it is installed; on Linux, where Codex is installed, fail when its sandbox cannot start (codex sandbox -- true: bwrap cannot make its namespaces, as under Ubuntu 24.04's AppArmor), naming the cause and the fix, which pm doctor leaves to the user; and name a pm release newer than the pin, with its notes (or say the release list cannot be read), and the launcher's pins that no checkout on this machine pins, which pm upgrade prunes: both leave the exit code alone
    upgrade             first print the upgrade guide of each release the move crosses, after the pin up to this pm, oldest first, from the release notes this pm was built with (no network; it fails, writing nothing, when a crossed release's guide is missing); then move the pin in .pm/config.toml to this pm and rewrite every managed piece as it writes them (the fix pm doctor names for a changed one), removing the pre-package harness's and the ones an earlier pm wrote and this one retired (the records/ copy and guard workflows, the pre-commit section); writes files and prints the commit to make, never commits; then lists the clone's other worktrees whose checked-out branch pins another pm, where pm refuses every work-store command once the main checkout pins this one, each with 'merge or rebase it onto the pin move'; last, prune the launcher's pins (<data dir>/pm/pins/<X>/pm) that no checkout on this machine pins (each worktree of this clone and of every clone with a pm service unit), keeping each pin's sha256 so a later download is checked against it, and pruning none when any of those cannot be read
    uninstall           remove pm's pieces from this worktree (hook entries, .gitignore block, .pm/, an earlier pm's workflows and git hook sections in .pm/hooks) and the clone's and machine's setup (the store checkout, records/ links, records workspaces and their drafts/ branches and an earlier pm's sparse checkouts in every worktree, refused while a workspace has uncommitted edits; the pm service, this clone's Codex writable_roots, pm's lines in .git/info/exclude, a core.hooksPath an earlier pm pointed at .pm/hooks, the work store (refused while it holds what the remote lacks; with the pm service stopped, it runs pm service run for that check)); keeps the records branch and the remote's work store (refs/pm/work); never commits
    init                install pm: the repo's files on first install (--site-url sets the public site link, --listen and --access who reaches it), then this clone, this worktree and the pm service (PORT=<n> sets its port, and on a first install the config's; PORT=<n> pm service install moves only the service's); session start runs it; never commits on the code branch
    push                sync the work store, summarize today and push the records branch; what the pm service runs every 10 minutes, not a session command
    where               list every location with its state: the store, this checkout and its records/ (the main checkout's link to the store, or a worktree's records workspace: its branch, uncommitted files, conflict markers and how far it is behind the records branch), the work store (in a checkout whose pin is not the version the clone's pm service runs, the mismatch and the fix: merge or rebase onto main's pin move, or pm service restart), the Codex sandbox roots, the pm service, the last push, and the site; 'pm where records' prints only the store's path
    clean               list each worktree with keep or remove and the reason; --apply removes the agent worktrees (.claude/worktrees/) that no live session owns and whose work is saved, and the interactive-mode files of sessions idle for a day
    interactive         turn the owner-request Stop check off for this session, so asks in chat pass, until pm autopilot, and print the interactive request rule; run it only when the owner asks
    autopilot           turn the owner-request Stop check back on for this session, the default, and print the autopilot request rule; a no-op in autopilot
    export              print every item of the work store, one JSON object per line, ordered by id; pm init --import reads it in another clone
    version             print this pm's version, dev for an untagged build; outside any repo too
    prime               pm's rules, then the rule for asking the owner in this session's mode, then pm init, pm where and pm show: the context a session starts with; the SessionStart hooks run --rules 1 to --rules 3, --request-rule and --state, and an agent may run it by hand
    hook                what a runtime hook runs: the hook input JSON on stdin; installs nothing
    commit              commit your hand edits to records, named by path, on the records branch

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit

pm runs the pm version the repo pins in .pm/config.toml: another version's release binary, downloaded once; a pin below 0.2.0 (a retired Python release) is refused. pm where names the version running and why.
===== pm show --help
usage: pm show [-h] [--json] [--project NAME] [--sprint ID] [--record PATH]
               [--section NAME] [--refresh-inbox]

Project state, level by level. Without a flag, the top level: push failures, tasks other live sessions hold, the site, the repo's pm feedback doc, and per open project a line with each open owner request and undelivered reply. Each level names the command for the next: --project, then --sprint, then --record, then --record with --section. pm show ID [--json] prints one item of any type instead: its fields, holder, blockers, children, needs and comments (pm show ID --help).

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --json           every level's data as one JSON object
  --project NAME   one open project (its name or id): its goal, owner requests in full, the repo's pm feedback doc, open sprints with their tasks, and last decisions
  --sprint ID      one sprint's frame, findings and tasks
  --record PATH    the record to read (a path, sprint id or name, project name or design slug); alone, its outline: header ids and each section with its line count
  --section NAME   with --record: print that one section, heading included
  --refresh-inbox  first point this session's open requests at its current inbox ($CLAUDE_CODE_MESSAGING_SOCKET); the session-start hook passes it, since a resumed session binds a new one
===== pm day --help
usage: pm day [-h] {summarize} ...

positional arguments:
  {summarize}
    summarize  generate today's Today summary with claude -p or codex exec (summary_runtime); the pm service runs it

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
===== pm day summarize --help
usage: pm day summarize [-h] [--dry-run]

Summarize today's activity (what the day page shows, plus the records committed today) into records/days/<today>.summary.json, committed on the records branch; the day page shows it as Today, labelled with the time it was generated. It asks the runtime that summary_runtime in .pm/config.toml names: claude (the default) runs claude -p --model claude-haiku-5-5, codex runs codex exec --model gpt-5.6-luna; each runs from an empty temp directory with no hooks, no project instructions and no saved session, for at most 180s. Set summary_runtime = "codex" in a repo whose machines have Codex and not Claude Code. Skips when nothing happened today or the activity's digest is unchanged since the last summary. Fails, writing nothing, when the runtime's CLI is missing, fails or times out; it never falls back to the other runtime. The pm service runs it every 10 minutes.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --dry-run   print the summary it would write; write nothing
===== pm finding --help
usage: pm finding [-h] {add} ...

positional arguments:
  {add}
    add       append a bullet to a sprint's Findings; the text as one argument, or with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm finding add --help
usage: pm finding add [-h] --sprint ID [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      [TEXT ...]

Append the finding, as a bullet, to the open sprint's Findings, with its numbers. Give the text as one quoted argument, or as the body: --text="…" for one plain line, or --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF. Refuses a text that starts with --, which is an option misspelt rather than a finding.

positional arguments:
  TEXT              the finding, as one quoted argument (words given apart are joined by spaces)

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID       the sprint's id
  --text-file PATH  the finding instead, read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the finding instead, inline, for one plain line only; backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm feedback --help
usage: pm feedback [-h] {add} ...

positional arguments:
  {add}
    add       append an entry to the repo's pm feedback doc; the text with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm feedback add --help
usage: pm feedback add [-h] [--project NAME] [--sprint ID] [--task ID]
                       [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT] [--session ID]

When pm got in the way (a confusing refusal, a missing command, a rule that cost time), say once what happened and what would have helped. Appends a dated entry with this session's id to the repo's one feedback doc, records/docs/pm-feedback.md, creating it on first use; --project, --sprint and --task tag the entry with what it is about. pm show and pm show --project link the doc.

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --project NAME    the project the feedback is about
  --sprint ID       the sprint the feedback is about
  --task ID         the task the feedback is about
  --text-file PATH  required: what happened and what would have helped; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
  --session ID      the session to record; default: this session's id from the environment
===== pm decision --help
usage: pm decision [-h] {add,need} ...

positional arguments:
  {add,need}
    add       append a decision to a project's or sprint's Decisions
    need      ask the owner for a decision under a sprint or task; its parts as flags

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm decision add --help
usage: pm decision add [-h] --decision TEXT --reason TEXT (--project NAME |
                       --sprint ID) [--until UNTIL] [--need ID | --confirmed]

Append a ::: decision block, dated today, to the Decisions of the named project or sprint: the --decision line, then the --reason line. Each is one line, in single quotes (in double quotes the shell runs a `code span` as a command). Choosing the scope: --project NAME if a later sprint must follow it; --sprint ID if it is about this sprint's own work; skip choices cheap to reverse. Source is agent unless --need or --confirmed. With --need, the decision is the owner's answer to that decision need: the block ends 'Answers `<need-id>`.', and the need is closed as answered with the same text unless the owner already closed it. Refuses while the need holds a site reply not delivered yet: read it with pm reply read <id> first.

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --decision TEXT  the decision, in one line
  --reason TEXT    why it holds, in one line
  --project NAME   a project decision, in this project's record: a later sprint must follow it
  --sprint ID      a sprint decision, in this sprint's record: it is about this sprint's own work
  --until UNTIL    a known condition to revisit the decision
  --need ID        source=owner: the decision answers this decision need, and closes it if it is open
  --confirmed      source=owner: the owner confirmed it
===== pm decision need --help
usage: pm decision need [-h] --question TEXT --fact TEXT --option LABEL TEXT
                        --cost LABEL TEXT --default LABEL REASON --title TITLE
                        (--sprint ID | --task ID)

Raise a decision need under a sprint (--sprint ID) or a task (--task ID). Its parts are flags, one line each, and stdin is not read: one --question, one or more --fact, two or more --option LABEL TEXT, one --cost LABEL TEXT for each option, and one --default LABEL REASON that names the option taken if the owner does not answer. Put each value in single quotes: in double quotes the shell runs a `code span` as a command. pm writes the description in one Markdown layout and refuses fewer than two options, a repeated label, an option without exactly one cost, a cost or default that names no option, and a sentence of more than 25 words. Record the answer with pm decision add --need, or close a small answer with pm need close.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --question TEXT       what the owner decides, as one question
  --fact TEXT           a fact the owner needs to decide; repeat it for each fact
  --option LABEL TEXT   a choice: its label (letters and digits) and what it does; repeat it for each option, two or more
  --cost LABEL TEXT     what the option with this label costs; one for each option
  --default LABEL REASON
                        the option taken if the owner does not answer, and why
  --title TITLE
  --sprint ID           the open sprint the decision belongs to
  --task ID             the open task the decision belongs to
===== pm action --help
usage: pm action [-h] {need} ...

positional arguments:
  {need}
    need      ask the owner to do something under a sprint or task, or to review a PR (--pr); description with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm action need --help
usage: pm action need [-h] [--title TITLE] [--task ID] [--pr URL]
                      [--sprint ID] [--focus FOCUS] [--design SLUG]
                      [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]

Raise an action under a sprint (--sprint ID) or a task (--task ID). The description (--text) says what to do and why. With --pr it is a PR review instead: every sprint named must be open with its delivery report written (Outcome and 'Against "Done when"') and committed; a GitHub PR must target the main branch, as a stacked PR's merge does not reach main (raise the review on the PR that does, or retarget it); another GitHub repo's PR (one whose OWNER/REPO the remote's URL does not name, or any when the remote is not on GitHub) must target that repo's default branch instead, and its review closes once it merges there; the review goes under the first and blocks its close until the PR merges and you close the review with pm need close <id> --reason "merged as <sha>"; the site's card links the PR, each sprint's record and delivery report, and the design pages named with --design plus those the sprints' records list, and shows the focus; --text then holds optional extra context. When a design page's covers: match a file this repo's PR changes (GitHub's pull request files API, through gh; a renamed file's old path counts too), it refuses until one of the sprints updated the page and links it in its Design pages, or waived it with pm design unchanged; the review's text then links each covering page. Close it with pm need close once you see it done.

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     required without --pr; with --pr, default: Review PR #<n>
  --task ID         without --pr: the open task the action belongs to, in place of --sprint; not allowed with --pr
  --pr URL          the pull request to review; requires --sprint and --focus
  --sprint ID       without --pr: the open sprint the action belongs to (once); with --pr: an open sprint the PR delivers, its report written (repeatable)
  --focus FOCUS     with --pr: what to look at first: risky changes, open choices
  --design SLUG     with --pr: a design page behind the PR (repeatable, optional)
  --text-file PATH  without --pr, required: what the owner should do and why; with --pr, optional extra context; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm need --help
usage: pm need [-h] {edit,close} ...

positional arguments:
  {edit,close}
    edit        rewrite an open action's or decision need's body before the owner replies
    close       close a need that sets no rule: a decision need with its answer, an action once done, a PR review once merged, or any need as --dropped

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
===== pm need edit --help
usage: pm need edit [-h] [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT] [--question TEXT]
                    [--fact TEXT] [--option LABEL TEXT] [--cost LABEL TEXT]
                    [--default LABEL REASON]
                    need_id

Rewrite the body of an open need that holds no reply yet: an action's description (--text), or a decision need's parts, given as pm decision need takes them (--question, --fact, --option, --cost, --default; pm decision need --help), which pm lays out and checks the same way. The title stays. Refuses a closed need, a need that holds a reply (the owner answered the body it had: raise a new need and close this one with pm need close --dropped), and a PR review, whose card pm builds from its PR, sprints and focus.

positional arguments:
  need_id               the open need's id

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --text-file PATH      an action's new description: what the owner should do and why; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT           the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
  --question TEXT       a decision need's question
  --fact TEXT           a decision need's fact; repeat it for each fact
  --option LABEL TEXT   a decision need's choice; two or more
  --cost LABEL TEXT     what the option with this label costs; one for each option
  --default LABEL REASON
                        the option taken if the owner does not answer, and why
===== pm need close --help
usage: pm need close [-h] --reason REASON [--dropped] [--text-file PATH |
                     --text TEXT]
                     need_id

Close a need that sets no rule, with --reason; its kind picks the resolution. A decision need closes as no-decision with the owner's answer as the body, as they gave it (--text or --text-file); on a need the owner already closed, only the resolution and the reason (as a comment) are set, and no answer is needed. An answer that sets a rule is recorded with pm decision add --need instead; answers that set no rule: a name, a port, which of two equal files; if you are not sure, record a decision. An action closes as done once you see the owner did it. A PR review closes as done with --reason "merged as <sha>" once the PR is on main; a review closed without it, as when its PR merged into a stacked base, takes it later the same way: <sha> must be on the remote's main branch (pm fetches it first), and the old reason stays after the new one. For another GitHub repo's PR (see pm action need), <sha> must be the PR's merge commit into that repo's default branch, as gh gives it, at the close and at the restamp. A session's question closes as dismissed: its answer went to the session that asked. --dropped closes any open need as dismissed instead, with no answer: a [TEST] need, a replaced review, or a decision or action that became moot; pm sprint close skips a dismissed review. Except with --dropped or for a question, it refuses while the need holds a site reply not delivered yet: read it with pm reply read <id> first. If setting a decision need's resolution fails, run it again; it does not repeat the reason.

positional arguments:
  need_id           the need's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --reason REASON   why it closes, in a sentence: why the answer sets no rule, what showed you the action done (merged as <sha> for a review), or why it is dropped
  --dropped         close it as dismissed, with no answer: a [TEST] need, a replaced review, or one that became moot
  --text-file PATH  a decision need's answer, as the owner gave it; required for an open decision need; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm reply --help
usage: pm reply [-h] {read,add} ...

positional arguments:
  {read,add}
    read      print the owner's replies and reviews' merges that did not reach your session
    add       the owner's answer to an open need, at a shell, as a reply on the site writes; the answer with --text

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm reply read --help
usage: pm reply read [-h] [ID ...]

Print the requests' site replies not yet delivered and their reviews' PR merges not yet reported, with what to do next, and mark them delivered; it does not wait. The pm service pushes each reply and merge into the inbox of the session that raised the request when it can; what it could not deliver (the session had ended, or has no inbox) waits here and is flagged by pm show. Without ids, this session's open requests ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or $CODEX_SESSION_ID, as pm task claim takes it); with ids, those, closed ones too.

positional arguments:
  ID          a decision need, action or review (default: this session's)

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm reply add --help
usage: pm reply add [-h] (--text TEXT | --text-file FILE) ID

The owner's answer to an open need, at a shell: a reply, as a reply on the site writes. The need stays open; the session that raised it reads the reply and records it, which closes the need.

positional arguments:
  ID                the open need's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --text TEXT       the answer, as one argument
  --text-file FILE  the answer, from a file; - reads stdin, from a pipe or heredoc only
===== pm doc --help
usage: pm doc [-h] {new,rename} ...

positional arguments:
  {new,rename}
    new         create records/docs/<today>-<slug>.md; body with --text
    rename      rename a doc, its slug or title or both, and rewrite every record's links to it

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
===== pm doc new --help
usage: pm doc new [-h] --title TITLE (--sprint ID | --task ID |
                  --project NAME) [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                  slug

positional arguments:
  slug              what the doc is for, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --sprint ID       the sprint the doc belongs to
  --task ID         the task the doc belongs to
  --project NAME    the project the doc belongs to
  --text-file PATH  required: the doc's Markdown body; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm doc rename --help
usage: pm doc rename [-h] [--to NEW_SLUG] [--title TITLE] doc

Rename a doc in one records commit: --to moves records/docs/<date>-<slug>.md to records/docs/<date>-<new>.md (the date prefix stays), --title sets its title: header; give one or both. Every record's Markdown link to the doc (../docs/<name>.md, or <name>.md beside it; .html too; anchors and link titles kept; none in code) is rewritten to the new name, and a link whose text is the old title takes the new one. Old URLs get no redirect. The repo's feedback doc is not renamed.

positional arguments:
  doc            the doc: records/docs/<date>-<slug>.md, <date>-<slug>, or <slug> when one doc has it

options:
  -h, --help     show this help message and exit
  --to NEW_SLUG  the new slug, lowercase words joined by '-', without the date
  --title TITLE  the new title
===== pm design --help
usage: pm design [-h] {new,rename,unchanged} ...

positional arguments:
  {new,rename,unchanged}
    new                 create records/design/<slug>.md with every template section
    rename              rename a design page, its slug or title or both, and rewrite every record's links and waivers to it
    unchanged           record why a design page stays as it is although the sprint's PR changes code it covers

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm design new --help
usage: pm design new [-h] --title TITLE --project NAME slug

Create a design page with every section of the template, each with its prompt line and "None yet."; then edit it by hand. A page covers one area and holds its final state; decisions and plans go in the project or sprint record. Put no date in the slug. When a page grows to cover several areas, split it into sub pages and keep a short summary per area linking them. Name the code the page is about in its header, covers: [internal/records/**, prime.md]: path patterns relative to the repo root (doublestar globs: * within one path segment, ** as a whole segment for any depth). pm check fails on a pattern that matches no tracked file; pm action need --pr and pm sprint close refuse a PR that changes a covered file until the sprint updates the page or runs pm design unchanged.

positional arguments:
  slug            what the design is, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --project NAME  the project the design belongs to
===== pm design rename --help
usage: pm design rename [-h] [--to NEW_SLUG] [--title TITLE] slug

Rename a design page in one records commit: --to moves records/design/<slug>.md to records/design/<new>.md, --title sets its title: header; give one or both. Every record's Markdown link to the page (../design/<slug>.md, or <slug>.md from another design page; .html too; anchors and link titles kept; none in code) is rewritten to the new slug, a link whose text is the old title takes the new one, and each pm design unchanged waiver of the page names the new slug. Old URLs get no redirect. Refused while an open PR review need names the page (pm action need --design): close or dismiss it first.

positional arguments:
  slug           the design page's slug (records/design/<slug>.md)

options:
  -h, --help     show this help message and exit
  --to NEW_SLUG  the new slug, lowercase words joined by '-'
  --title TITLE  the new title
===== pm design unchanged --help
usage: pm design unchanged [-h] --sprint ID --reason REASON slug

Record, as a sprint decision of source agent, that the design page stays as it is for the sprint's code changes, and why. pm action need --pr and pm sprint close refuse a PR that changes a file a design page covers (its covers: header) until one of the PR's sprints either updated the page (its draft for the sprint, which pm commit makes in a worktree's records workspace, or a records commit of it since the sprint opened, and a link to it in the sprint record's Design pages section) or recorded this. pm sprint close --discard records the same for a page whose draft it drops. Use it when the change leaves what the page says true; when it does not, edit the page to the new state instead.

positional arguments:
  slug             the design page's slug (records/design/<slug>.md)

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID      the open sprint whose PR changes the covered code
  --reason REASON  why the page stays true, in one line
===== pm postmortem --help
usage: pm postmortem [-h] {new} ...

positional arguments:
  {new}
    new       create records/postmortems/<today>-<slug>.md with every template section

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm postmortem new --help
usage: pm postmortem new [-h] --title TITLE (--sprint ID | --project NAME)
                         slug

Create a postmortem with every section of the template, each with its prompt line and "None yet."; then write it by hand. Due for an incident that cost more than a day, or broke other sessions or the owner's view. Write it once the incident is fixed.

positional arguments:
  slug            what broke, lowercase words joined by '-'

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --sprint ID     the sprint the incident hit, open or closed
  --project NAME  the project the incident hit
===== pm project --help
usage: pm project [-h] {open,close,reopen,edit} ...

positional arguments:
  {open,close,reopen,edit}
    open                create a project epic and record; Goal with --text
    close               close a project epic at the committed records
    reopen              set a closed project open again; reason with --text
    edit                rename a project, open or closed; reason with --text

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm project open --help
usage: pm project open [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                       project

Create a project epic and its record, with the Goal from --text. The owner confirms the goal in their own words before you open the project; a request from the owner that states the goal confirms it.

positional arguments:
  project           the project's name

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  required: the project's Goal; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm project close --help
usage: pm project close [-h] project

Close a project epic at the committed records. Close every sprint first. Write the record's '## Outcome' first, by hand: the results against the goal in numbers, what was learned, what was retired, and links to the sprints' delivery reports.

positional arguments:
  project     the project's name

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm project reopen --help
usage: pm project reopen [-h] [--confirmed] [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                         project

Set a closed project open again: its work-store epic first, then one records commit that puts the record's '## Outcome' back to 'Not closed yet.', so the next pm project close needs a fresh one (the old text stays in git history), and adds the reopen, with the close reason it had, as a decision to the project's Decisions: source=agent, or source=owner with --confirmed. When the records step fails, the epic is closed again with its old close reason, at a new close time. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a project that is open. Its sprints stay closed; pm sprint reopen reopens one, or pm sprint open adds one.

positional arguments:
  project           the project's name

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --confirmed       source=owner: the owner asked for the reopen
  --text-file PATH  required: why the project reopens, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm project edit --help
usage: pm project edit [-h] --title TITLE [--confirmed] [--text-file PATH |
                       --text TEXT]
                       project

Give a project, open or closed, a new title: its work-store title, and its record's title header, in one records commit that also adds the rename, from the work store's old title, as a decision to the project's Decisions: source=agent, or source=owner with --confirmed. The work store is written first; when the records step fails, the old title is put back. The project's name, record path and links stay as they are. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses an empty title, and a title that both the work store and the record's header hold already; a header edited by hand to the new title while the work store still holds the old one (the drift pm check names) is renamed as any other.

positional arguments:
  project           the project's name

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     the new title
  --confirmed       source=owner: the owner asked for the rename
  --text-file PATH  required: why the title changes, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint --help
usage: pm sprint [-h] {open,close,edit,move,reopen} ...

positional arguments:
  {open,close,edit,move,reopen}
    open                create a sprint epic and record; frame with --text ('## Goal', '## Scope' with **In:**/**Out:**, '## Done when')
    close               close a sprint epic once its report is written, every task is closed and each PR review is closed as merged
    edit                rename an open sprint; reason with --text
    move                move an open sprint, with its tasks, frame, decisions and findings, to another open project; reason with --text
    reopen              set a closed sprint open again; reason with --text

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm sprint open --help
usage: pm sprint open [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      project

Create a sprint epic and its record from the frame, then print the design pages the project's record lists: slug, title and link, or missing for a slug no design page has.

positional arguments:
  project           project record name, e.g. pm-harness

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  required: the frame, '## Goal', '## Scope' and '## Done when' sections; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint close --help
usage: pm sprint close [-h] [--merged SHA] [--pr URL] [--discard SLUG]
                       [--reason REASON]
                       sprint_id

Close a sprint epic at the committed records. Refuses until the Delivery report is written and every task is closed, and each PR review naming the sprint is closed with pm need close <id> --reason "merged as <sha>" once its PR is on main; it asks GitHub only for the files each of this repo's PRs changes, and refuses, as pm action need --pr does, while a design page covering one is neither updated nor waived with pm design unchanged (with --merged and no --pr, the files a merge commit brought against its first parent; a commit with one parent, a squash or rebase merge, is refused there, as its delivery cannot be told, so give --pr). A PR an agent merged has no review: --merged SHA [--pr URL] gives its merge commit, which must be on the remote's main branch (pm fetches it first), and stamps 'Merged as <sha>.' ('Merged as <sha> (PR #N).' with --pr); with another GitHub repo's PR as --pr (see pm action need), SHA must be its merge commit into that repo's default branch, as gh gives it, and the stamp names it OWNER/REPO#N, as a review's close does; it refuses a sprint that holds a PR review, whose close stamps the merge instead. Then it publishes the sprint's design pages: every page a draft commit of this clone tags with the sprint (pm commit drafts them in a worktree's records workspace), from every worktree and every drafts/ branch whose worktree is gone, merged onto the records branch. It refuses, changing nothing, while this worktree's records workspace has uncommitted edits to a page the sprint links; while a draft overlaps what the records branch published since the draft's base, or another draft of the sprint, naming the commits or the branch and the fix (a pm write in that worktree brings the records branch in, leaving conflict markers to resolve and pm commit); and while a page to publish has a covers: pattern that matches no file on the remote's main (fetched): fix the pattern, or merge the PR that adds the file first. --discard SLUG --reason "…" drops the sprint's drafts of that page instead, puts back this workspace's uncommitted edits to it, and records the reason as pm design unchanged does; repeat both for several pages. One records commit then holds the pages, the 'Merged as' stamp in the Outcome for each review, and the waivers; the drafts lose the published pages, a drafts/ branch with no worktree left empty is deleted, and the close reason names the commit. A sprint with no PR publishes the same way. Run again after a stop, it finds nothing left to publish. Only the Outcome's first paragraph becomes the close reason.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id        the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --merged SHA     the merge commit of the sprint's PR, merged with no PR review: a commit on the remote's main branch, or another repo's PR's merge commit
  --pr URL         with --merged: the merged pull request, named in the stamp
  --discard SLUG   a design page whose drafts for the sprint are dropped, not published; one --reason with each
  --reason REASON  with --discard: why the page stays as it is, in one line
===== pm sprint edit --help
usage: pm sprint edit [-h] --title TITLE [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      sprint_id

Give an open sprint a new title: its work-store title, which keeps its 'Sprint <n>: ' prefix, and its record's title header, in one records commit that also adds the rename as a source=agent decision to the sprint's Decisions. The work store is written first; when the records step fails, the old title is put back. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a closed sprint, an unchanged title, and a title that carries its own 'Sprint <n>: ' prefix. A scope change is still a sprint decision.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id         the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     the new title, without 'Sprint <n>: '
  --text-file PATH  required: why the title changes, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint move --help
usage: pm sprint move [-h] --to PROJECT [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                      sprint_id

Move an open sprint to another open project. Its id stays, so its tasks, needs and holders stay as they are and the old id shows the sprint in its new place; it takes the project's next sprint number, and its record moves to records/sprints/<project>-<number>.md. The work store is written first: the new parent and number, and a move note on the sprint that keeps its old project and number, so the old project never reuses that number and the old record path still finds the record. Then one records commit renames the record and adds the move as a source=agent decision to both projects' records. If the records step fails or is cut short, run the same command again: it finds the move in the work store and writes the records step alone, with the reason and the date (UTC) the move note holds, so a rerun on a clone whose records have not yet synced another clone's finished step writes that same step, which the records sync then drops. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a closed sprint, a closed project, the project the sprint is in, and a move to another project while the last move's records step has not run.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id         the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --to PROJECT      the open project the sprint moves to, by record name, e.g. pm-harness
  --text-file PATH  required: why the sprint moves, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm sprint reopen --help
usage: pm sprint reopen [-h] [--confirmed] [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                        sprint_id

Set a closed sprint open again: its work-store epic first, then one records commit that puts the record's '## Delivery report' back to 'Not closed yet.' in both parts, so the next pm sprint close needs a fresh report (the old one stays in git history), and adds the reopen, with the close reason it had, as a decision to the sprint's Decisions: source=agent, or source=owner with --confirmed. When the records step fails, the epic is closed again with its old close reason, at a new close time. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines. Refuses a sprint that is open, and a sprint whose project is closed: reopen the project first with pm project reopen. Its tasks stay closed; add new work with pm task add.

positional arguments:
  sprint_id         the sprint's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --confirmed       source=owner: the owner asked for the reopen
  --text-file PATH  required: why the sprint reopens, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task --help
usage: pm task [-h] {add,close,claim,move,ready,edit,release} ...

positional arguments:
  {add,close,claim,move,ready,edit,release}
    add                 create a task in an open sprint, or a sub-task (--task); description with --text (optional)
    close               close a task with a reason naming its commit, or drop it
    claim               claim a task for this agent session
    move                move a task to another open sprint, or into a sprint from directly under a project; reason with --text
    ready               list the tasks an agent can claim now
    edit                set a task's title or description; description with --text
    release             clear the holder of a task this session holds

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm task add --help
usage: pm task add [-h] (--sprint ID | --task TASK) --title TITLE
                   [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]

Create a task in an open sprint. pm task add --task TASK --title TITLE, in place of --sprint, adds a sub-task under the open task TASK instead (pm task add --task TASK --help).

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID       the open sprint's id
  --task TASK       the open task a sub-task is a part of, in place of --sprint (pm task add --task TASK --help)
  --title TITLE
  --text-file PATH  optional: the task's description; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task close --help
usage: pm task close [-h] [--reason REASON] [--commit REF] [--dropped] task_id

Close a task as done, with a required --reason saying what was done. The reason ends with what holds the work: '(commit <hash>)' for HEAD if it was committed after the task started, else the --commit given: a commit of this repo, OWNER/REPO@SHA for another repo's commit, or a PR URL, the last two resolved with gh; one that does not resolve, or a PR closed without merging, is refused. Without --commit, a warning when HEAD is older than the task or the working tree has uncommitted changes. --dropped closes it as not done instead (resolution dismissed, shown as dropped and left out of a sprint's task counts), with a required --reason and no commit; a task it blocks no longer waits on it. Refuses a task that another live session holds, as pm task claim does; that session closes it or releases it with pm task release.

positional arguments:
  task_id          the task's id

options:
  -h, --help       show this help message and exit
  --reason REASON  required: what was done; with --dropped, why it is not done
  --commit REF     what holds the work: a commit of this repo, OWNER/REPO@SHA or a PR URL (default: HEAD, if newer than the task)
  --dropped        close it as not done: no commit, a reason required
===== pm task claim --help
usage: pm task claim [-h] [--session ID] task_id

Claim an open task: make the session (--session, else $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID or Codex's $CODEX_SESSION_ID, else its $CODEX_THREAD_ID's root thread; with both runtimes' set, that of the nearest Claude Code or Codex process pm runs under) its holder, with the time. Refuses when another live session holds it: one whose transcript was written in the last 30 minutes. A subagent shares its session's id, so it may claim what its session holds. Refuses in the main checkout, since agents change code only in a worktree of their own, and says how to make one. On the session's first claim in the task's sprint (no other task there held or closed by it; for a task directly under a project, in the project), prints the design pages the sprint's record, then the project's, list: slug, title and link, or missing for a slug no design page has.

positional arguments:
  task_id       the task's id

options:
  -h, --help    show this help message and exit
  --session ID  the session to record; default: this session's id from the environment
===== pm task move --help
usage: pm task move [-h] --to SPRINT_ID [--text-file PATH | --text TEXT]
                    task_id

Move an open task to another open sprint and record the scope change as a source=agent decision in the sprint it leaves. A task filed directly under a project (the site's Not in a sprint) moves into one of that project's open sprints, and the decision records the scope added in the sprint it joins. The reason (--text) states why, on at least two lines.

positional arguments:
  task_id           the task's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --to SPRINT_ID    the open sprint the task moves to
  --text-file PATH  required: why the task moves, on at least two lines; read from PATH, or with - from stdin as a quoted heredoc: --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF
  --text TEXT       the same body inline, for one plain line only; several lines, backticks, $ or quotes go in --text-file
===== pm task ready --help
usage: pm task ready [-h] [--sprint ID] [--json]

The tasks an agent can claim now: open, under open ancestors, held by no live session, with no open blocker of their own or an ancestor's. By sprint number, then id; tasks directly under a project last. A task whose holder is not live is ready and marked a stale holder; pm task claim takes it over.

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
  --sprint ID  only the tasks under this sprint
  --json       the ready tasks as a JSON array of items
===== pm task edit --help
usage: pm task edit [-h] [--title TITLE] [--text TEXT | --text-file FILE] ID

Set a task's title, its description (the body: --text, or --text-file - <<'EOF' … EOF), or both. A scope change is still pm task move or a sprint decision.

positional arguments:
  ID                the task's id

options:
  -h, --help        show this help message and exit
  --title TITLE     the new title
  --text TEXT       the new description, as one argument
  --text-file FILE  the new description, from a file; - reads stdin, from a pipe or heredoc only
===== pm task release --help
usage: pm task release [-h] ID

Clear the holder of a task this session ($CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, $CODEX_SESSION_ID or $CODEX_THREAD_ID, as pm task claim takes it) holds.

positional arguments:
  ID          the task's id

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
