===== pm record --help
usage: pm record [-h] {link} ...

positional arguments:
  {link}
    link      print a record's page URL on the served site; the only link to give for a record

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm record link --help
usage: pm record link [-h] [--local] target

Print the URL of a record's page on the site the pm service serves on localhost:$PORT (default: the installed service's port, else the port in .pm/config.toml), printed with the repo's site_url instead when .pm/config.toml sets one; --local prints the localhost URL even then, for a check on this machine that the public site's login would stop (a headless browser). The pm service's pages follow the records within 10 s and state their data's age, so no render step is needed. Fails with the command that fixes it when nothing serves there, or when what answers is not the pm service for this store.

positional arguments:
  target      a sprint or project id, a sprint name (<project>-<n>), a project name, a design slug, or a record path (records/<…>.md; records/ and .md optional)

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --local     print http://127.0.0.1:<port>/…, the URL the service answers on here, even when site_url is set
===== pm check --help
usage: pm check [-h]

Check that every record renders with the work store, writing nothing; pm commit runs the same check. In a worktree with a records workspace it checks the published records with this workspace's drafts over them. It names each project record, open or closed, whose title header differs from its work-store title, on a line of its own after the design report, with the pm project edit --title, quoted for the shell, that carries the header's title to the work store (it takes the reason as its body, as every rename does); this is a report, and neither pm check nor pm commit fails on it. It lists the tracked source files no design page covers: those the path patterns of design_sources in .pm/config.toml match (e.g. design_sources = ["internal/**", "prime.md"]; unset, no list), and fails on a design_sources pattern that matches no tracked file. It fails on a design page's covers: pattern that matches no file where the page holds it: a published page's on the remote's main (its remote-tracking branch, fetched when a pattern matches nothing there), since a page publishes at its sprint's close once its code is on main; a draft page's in this worktree's tracked files. A published pattern main lacks while a draft of the page in this clone no longer holds it (a merged PR removed the file, its sprint not closed yet) does not fail: it is listed as waiting on that sprint's close, naming the sprint. A drafts/ branch pm cannot read (a commit made by hand) is named in a warning and left out. A malformed pattern fails.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm sync --help
usage: pm sync [-h]

Sync the work store with the repo's remote now: the pm service pulls, resolves conflicts by the merge rules and pushes, as it does every 10 minutes. A conflict no rule settles fails, names the item and field, and leaves the store as it was.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service --help
usage: pm service [-h] {install,status,restart,stop,logs} ...

The pm service: one supervised background process per clone, `pm service run` in the main checkout. It
holds the work store, which every pm command reaches through its socket, .pm/run/work.sock, and serves the
site live from the records store and the work store (a page is at most 10 s behind them), delivers
the owner's site replies and reviewed PRs' merges (GitHub polled every 60 s) into the sessions that
raised them, and syncs the work store, pushes today's summary and the records branch every
600 s, the first push 600 s after it starts. Sessions push neither.

Supervisor: it starts the service at login and again after a crash. A launchd agent with KeepAlive on
macOS (~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.plist); a systemd user service with Restart=always
on Linux ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.service, ~/.config by default). On a
machine with neither there is no service, and install refuses.

Unit: runs the installed pm (`<bin dir>/pm service run`, the bin dir $PM_BIN_DIR, else ~/.local/bin; pm init
copies pm there) in the main checkout, with the PATH install ran with (git must be on it, and uv
for a Python pin) and PORT. The installed pm runs the version the main checkout pins: another Go version's release binary, a Python version
through uv.

Port: $PORT, else the installed unit's port, else `port` in .pm/config.toml. Installing again keeps the unit's
port. A second clone of the repo on this machine needs its own: PORT=<n> pm service install.

State and logs, in <main checkout>/.pm/run/ (never committed): service.log (pm service logs), push.json
(each push step's last outcome), push.log (a line per step per run), push.lock (one push at a time).

Health (pm service status, pm where): the supervisor holds the unit, and the site answers on its port
with X-PM-Store naming this clone's store and X-PM-Version naming this pm's build. Status also
flags a push step that failed or has not succeeded for 1800 s.

Stale build: every 1 s the service rereads the pin in .pm/config.toml; once it pins another version (a
pull after pm upgrade) the service exits and the supervisor starts the installed pm again, which runs the new
pin. A service that answers on another build than the running pm, the pinned one, is stale in pm where, pm
service status and pm doctor. Fix: pm init (it copies this pm into the bin dir when another is there, then
installs the service); when the installed pm already is this build, pm service install or pm service restart.
A unit that runs another program than the installed pm (Python pm's unit runs the pm uv tool's interpreter)
is stale there too, and session start leaves it: pm service install rewrites it.

Agents: pm prime carries pm where's service line and push state, and pm show warns when a push needs
attention. When the service is down, run pm service restart; if that fails, raise an action for the
owner (pm action need) and add a bug task (pm task add). pm init installs the service; pm service stop
stops it and keeps it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init;
pm uninstall stops it and removes its unit.

positional arguments:
  {install,status,restart,stop,logs}
    install             install and start this clone's service (launchd on macOS, systemd on Linux), or update it; a no-op once installed and current
    status              whether the service is up and its site answers for this store, and its pushes; non-zero when either needs attention
    restart             restart the service and wait for its site to answer; when it fails, raise an action for the owner and add a bug task
    stop                stop this clone's service and keep it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init
    logs                print the end of the service's log, <main checkout>/.pm/run/service.log

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit

pm service --help --all also lists the machinery commands: what the runtimes' hooks, the scheduler and the pm service run, not agents.
===== pm service --help --all
usage: pm service [-h] {install,status,restart,stop,logs,run} ...

The pm service: one supervised background process per clone, `pm service run` in the main checkout. It
holds the work store, which every pm command reaches through its socket, .pm/run/work.sock, and serves the
site live from the records store and the work store (a page is at most 10 s behind them), delivers
the owner's site replies and reviewed PRs' merges (GitHub polled every 60 s) into the sessions that
raised them, and syncs the work store, pushes today's summary and the records branch every
600 s, the first push 600 s after it starts. Sessions push neither.

Supervisor: it starts the service at login and again after a crash. A launchd agent with KeepAlive on
macOS (~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.plist); a systemd user service with Restart=always
on Linux ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/local.pm.<dir>.<hash>.service, ~/.config by default). On a
machine with neither there is no service, and install refuses.

Unit: runs the installed pm (`<bin dir>/pm service run`, the bin dir $PM_BIN_DIR, else ~/.local/bin; pm init
copies pm there) in the main checkout, with the PATH install ran with (git must be on it, and uv
for a Python pin) and PORT. The installed pm runs the version the main checkout pins: another Go version's release binary, a Python version
through uv.

Port: $PORT, else the installed unit's port, else `port` in .pm/config.toml. Installing again keeps the unit's
port. A second clone of the repo on this machine needs its own: PORT=<n> pm service install.

State and logs, in <main checkout>/.pm/run/ (never committed): service.log (pm service logs), push.json
(each push step's last outcome), push.log (a line per step per run), push.lock (one push at a time).

Health (pm service status, pm where): the supervisor holds the unit, and the site answers on its port
with X-PM-Store naming this clone's store and X-PM-Version naming this pm's build. Status also
flags a push step that failed or has not succeeded for 1800 s.

Stale build: every 1 s the service rereads the pin in .pm/config.toml; once it pins another version (a
pull after pm upgrade) the service exits and the supervisor starts the installed pm again, which runs the new
pin. A service that answers on another build than the running pm, the pinned one, is stale in pm where, pm
service status and pm doctor. Fix: pm init (it copies this pm into the bin dir when another is there, then
installs the service); when the installed pm already is this build, pm service install or pm service restart.
A unit that runs another program than the installed pm (Python pm's unit runs the pm uv tool's interpreter)
is stale there too, and session start leaves it: pm service install rewrites it.

Agents: pm prime carries pm where's service line and push state, and pm show warns when a push needs
attention. When the service is down, run pm service restart; if that fails, raise an action for the
owner (pm action need) and add a bug task (pm task add). pm init installs the service; pm service stop
stops it and keeps it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init;
pm uninstall stops it and removes its unit.

positional arguments:
  {install,status,restart,stop,logs,run}
    install             install and start this clone's service (launchd on macOS, systemd on Linux), or update it; a no-op once installed and current
    status              whether the service is up and its site answers for this store, and its pushes; non-zero when either needs attention
    restart             restart the service and wait for its site to answer; when it fails, raise an action for the owner and add a bug task
    stop                stop this clone's service and keep it stopped, at login and at session start, until pm service restart or pm init
    logs                print the end of the service's log, <main checkout>/.pm/run/service.log
    run                 the service's process, run by its supervisor: serve the site on $PORT at the config's listen address (127.0.0.1 when unset) and push every 10 minutes

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm service install --help
usage: pm service install [-h]

Install the pm service under the machine's supervisor, which starts it at login and restarts it after a crash: a launchd agent with KeepAlive on macOS, a systemd user service on Linux; refused on a machine with neither. The unit runs the installed pm (refused unless it is this pm's build: run pm init) with the current PATH (git must be on it) and serves on $PORT, else the port it was installed with, else the port in .pm/config.toml; give a second clone of the repo its own port once with PORT=<n> pm service install. Installing again rewrites a changed unit and restarts a service on another build. It waits 15 s for the site to answer for this store and fails when it does not (another clone's service on the port, say). pm init runs it.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service status --help
usage: pm service status [-h]

Print the service's health line (as pm where does), each push step's last outcome, every push problem and the log's path. Up means the supervisor holds the unit and the site answers on its port with X-PM-Store naming this store and X-PM-Version naming this pm's build; a push problem is a step whose last run failed or that has not succeeded for 1800 s. Exits non-zero when the service is down, stale or a push needs attention; each line names the command that fixes it.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service restart --help
usage: pm service restart [-h]

Restart the installed service, or load it when the supervisor does not hold it (enabling it first when pm service stop stopped it), then wait 15 s for the site to answer for this store. The fix when pm where shows the service down. Refused when it is not installed (run pm service install); when it fails, read pm service logs, raise an action for the owner (pm action need) and add a bug task (pm task add).

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service stop --help
usage: pm service stop [-h]

Disable the service at its supervisor and stop it (systemd: systemctl --user disable --now; launchd: launchctl disable, then bootout), then wait 15 s until neither the work store's socket nor the site answers, and say so; fails naming what still answers (a pm service run started by hand). The supervisor's disabled state is the record of the stop: the supervisor starts it neither at login nor after a crash, session start (pm init --session-start) leaves it stopped and its state names pm service restart, and pm where, pm service status and pm doctor show it stopped. Meanwhile every pm command that reads or writes work items refuses; pm uninstall still checks for unsynced work, through a pm service run of its own. A typed pm init or pm service restart enables and starts it again. Refused when it is not installed.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm service logs --help
usage: pm service logs [-h] [-n LINES]

Print the end of <main checkout>/.pm/run/service.log, where the service writes stdout and stderr: a line per request, refresh and reply write with its timings, and any error that stopped it. The push's own log is .pm/run/push.log beside it.

options:
  -h, --help         show this help message and exit
  -n, --lines LINES  how many lines (default 50)
===== pm service run --help
usage: pm service run [-h]

Serve the site on $PORT (default: port in .pm/config.toml) at listen in .pm/config.toml (127.0.0.1 when unset; beside another specific address 127.0.0.1 too), letting in each request as access says (pm init --help); refused when listen is not loopback or site_url is set and access is not: a page is at most 10 s behind the records and the work store and states its data's age; an open page never reloads itself but shows within ~10 s that newer data exists, loaded on reload. Every 10 minutes, the first 10 after start, run pm push. Exits once .pm/config.toml pins another pm version, so the supervisor starts the installed pm again, which runs the new pin. The supervisor runs it; run it by hand only to debug, or on another PORT.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm doctor --help
usage: pm doctor [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm upgrade --help
usage: pm upgrade [-h] [--to X]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --to X      the version to move to: the running pm's (the default), or another, which the installed pm launches to make the move; without it pm upgrade refuses a pin newer than the running pm (--to the pin rewrites the pieces at it)
===== pm uninstall --help
usage: pm uninstall [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm init --help
usage: pm init [-h] [--session-start] [--site-url URL] [--listen ADDR]
               [--access {cloudflare,none}] [--import-bd FILE | --import FILE]

Install pm, doing only what is missing. Repo, on first install only (no .pm/config.toml yet): write .pm/ (config.toml, README.md, .gitignore), pm's hook entries in .claude/settings.json and .codex/hooks.json, the Codex rule .codex/rules/pm.rules (pm runs outside Codex's sandbox), Codex's auto mode in .codex/config.toml (approval_policy on-request, approvals_reviewer auto_review, sandbox_mode workspace-write; Codex reads it in a repo you trust; a key you set to another value stays yours: pm doctor does not name it, pm upgrade keeps it, pm uninstall leaves it) and pm's .gitignore lines; create the records branch with an empty store and push it when the remote has none; print the commit to make. After that pm init leaves the repo's files alone: pm doctor reports a changed or missing piece and pm upgrade rewrites it. Clone and worktree, every run: copy this pm into the bin dir when another is there, take out Beads' hook entries and CLAUDE.md block, unset a core.hooksPath that names .pm/hooks, where an earlier pm kept its git hooks, while nothing but pm's sections is there (pm installs none; a hooks path elsewhere, or one holding hooks of yours, stays), list .pm/store/ and .pm/run/ in .git/info/exclude, check out the records store at <main checkout>/.pm/store/records if missing, give this worktree its records/ (in the main checkout the link to the store, with the store in its .claude/settings.local.json; in any other worktree its records workspace, a worktree of branch drafts/<worktree's name> at <worktree>/records in place of an earlier pm's link, where its sessions edit records) and turn off the sparse checkout an earlier pm set once HEAD and the index track no records/, add the clone's .git and the stores to the writable roots of $CODEX_HOME/config.toml, then install the pm service (pm service install) and have it attach the work store, which only the service opens (cloned from the remote's refs/pm/work, or created and pushed). Session start runs it in every worktree, and starts an installed service that does not answer; once all is set up it prints 'already set up'. Site port: a new repo's config gets $PORT, else the first free port from 8000 up that no pm service unit on this machine names; a clone's service serves on $PORT, else its unit's port, else the config's. Refuses, writing nothing, when another server holds that port, and names a free one: PORT=<n> pm init. .pm/config.toml, tracked: version (the pm every session must run; pm upgrade moves it), remote and main_branch (origin and its default branch), port (the site port), site_url (--site-url), listen (--listen) and access (--access). The site is three settings: listen is the address it binds, 127.0.0.1 (this machine only) when unset; site_url is the link pm prints and the one Host besides this machine's it serves; access is how a request from beyond this machine gets in, required once listen is not loopback or site_url is set (the service does not start without it). A request is local, and served, only when its TCP peer is loopback, its Host is localhost or 127.0.0.1 and Cloudflare's edge did not forward it (Cf-Connecting-IP); any other must name site_url's host, or come through Cloudflare's edge. access = "cloudflare": with access_team (the Cloudflare Access team domain, <team>.cloudflareaccess.com) and access_aud (the Access application's AUD tag), set by hand, such a request is served only when its Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion verifies against them (RS256 by the team's keys, aud, iss, unexpired), and every one is refused while either is missing. access = "none": the network is the gate; such a request reads the site but may not reply (its pages show no reply form, and a POST /reply is refused), since pm cannot tell the owner from anyone else on it; a request Cloudflare's edge forwarded is refused. A config with access_team and no access gets access = "cloudflare" from pm init. pm doctor names a missing key, and the service reads them when it starts.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --session-start       what session start runs, without $PORT: install the pm service when it is missing and start an installed one that does not answer, so a session in a clone brings its service up; one pm service stop stopped is left stopped and reported (pm service restart starts it); a running one, a stale one included, is left as it is and reported (pm service restart restarts it)
  --site-url URL        the site's public base URL (a tunnel to the pm service), written to site_url in .pm/config.toml for you to commit: every link pm prints (pm record link, pm show, pm where) uses it instead of http://localhost:<port>, and the site serves its host besides localhost, behind access; '' clears it
  --listen ADDR         the IP address the site binds, written to listen in .pm/config.toml for you to commit: 0.0.0.0 serves every interface, a LAN address one (the service binds 127.0.0.1 beside it); a non-loopback one needs access; '' clears it (127.0.0.1); pm service restart applies it
  --access {cloudflare,none}
                        how a request from beyond this machine gets in, written to access in .pm/config.toml for you to commit: cloudflare verifies a Cloudflare Access token (access_team, access_aud), none lets the network read and replies come only from this machine; required with a non-loopback listen or a site_url
  --import-bd FILE      only import the bd export in FILE into this clone's work store, through the pm service, as one commit; refuses a store that holds any item, and an export it cannot map; nothing else runs
  --import FILE         only import the pm export in FILE (what pm export printed in another clone) into this clone's work store, ids kept, as --import-bd does
===== pm push --help
usage: pm push [-h]

Sync the work store with the remote, then summarize today (pm day summarize only when today's activity changed), then push the records branch when the store is ahead of <remote>/records: fetch, rebase onto it if it moved (under the store lock; a rebase that stops is aborted, leaving the store as it was), push. Each step has a 120s timeout; a second run while one holds the lock exits at once. Each step's outcome goes to <clone>/.pm/run/push.json (read by pm show, pm where, pm service status and the site) and <clone>/.pm/run/push.log, a line per step.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm where --help
usage: pm where [-h] [{records}]

positional arguments:
  {records}   print only this location's path

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm clean --help
usage: pm clean [-h] [--apply]

List every worktree of this clone with keep or remove and the reason; a dry run unless --apply. Only an agent worktree, under <main checkout>/.claude/worktrees/, is ever removed; never the main checkout, the records store (.pm/store/records), a records workspace on its own or the worktree pm clean runs in. Owned, so kept: a worktree Claude Code locked for a live process (`claude <kind> <name> (pid N start T)` in .git/worktrees/<name>/locked; the lock is stale when pid N is gone or, on Linux, started at another time than T; on macOS a live pid owns it), a worktree someone else locked, and one a live session used: an entry of a Claude Code transcript (<config dir>/projects/) or a Codex rollout ($CODEX_HOME/sessions/) written in the last 30 minutes whose directory is in the worktree or whose tool call names the worktree's path (a subagent runs in its parent's directory). Removed, when clean (no change, no untracked file; its ignored files go with it, as git worktree remove deletes them) and holding no other worktree but its records workspace (<worktree>/records, which goes first with its drafts/ branch, and keeps the worktree while it has uncommitted edits or a draft commit): one whose commits are on <remote>/<main branch> (its branch deleted too), one whose branch a PR merged into <main branch> took at its tip (a squash merge, asked of gh; kept when gh is missing or fails; its branch deleted too), and one whose branch has nothing beyond where it was pushed: its upstream, unless that is the main branch, else <remote>/<branch> (its branch kept, as it is not merged). Everything else is kept: uncommitted changes, commits never pushed. The branches compare with <remote>/<main branch> as last fetched: run git fetch first. --apply takes a stale lock off, runs git worktree remove (never --force, so git refuses a worktree that changed meanwhile; for a worktree whose directory is gone it drops git's entry), and deletes a merged branch at the tip it judged. It refuses, removing nothing, when a recent transcript cannot be read. After the worktrees it lists each session's interactive-mode file (<main checkout>/.pm/run/sessions/<session id>, which pm interactive writes): removed once its session has been idle for a day (no transcript or rollout written in the last 24 hours), as a session that crashed leaves it; a session that ends removes its own (pm hook session-end). Exits 1 when a removal failed, naming why on its line.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
  --apply     remove the worktrees the dry run lists as remove; without it pm clean changes nothing
===== pm interactive --help
usage: pm interactive [-h]

Turn the owner-request Stop check (pm hook owner-request) off for this session alone: its stops pass without the judge, so asking the owner in chat is fine, until pm autopilot. It writes <main checkout>/.pm/run/sessions/<session id>, the session from the environment (CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, or Codex's session), and refuses when none is set; then it prints the request rule of interactive mode, which the SessionStart hook pm prime --request-rule also gives the session from then on, after a compaction too. The session's end removes the mode (pm hook session-end), so a session resumed after it ended, a claude -p or codex exec run resumed included, starts in autopilot. Run it only when the owner asks for interactive mode; nothing enforces that, so the rule is the agent's to keep. The uncommitted-records check (pm hook stop) stays on. The mode is local to the clone, shared by its worktrees and never synced; pm clean removes one a crashed session left, once it has been idle for a day.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm autopilot --help
usage: pm autopilot [-h]

Turn the owner-request Stop check back on for this session: it removes <main checkout>/.pm/run/sessions/<session id>, which pm interactive wrote, the session from the environment, and refuses when none is set; then it prints the request rule of autopilot. Autopilot is every session's default; in a session already in it, nothing changes and the first line says so. Run it when the owner asks.

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm export --help
usage: pm export [-h] [--store DIR]

Print every item of this clone's work store, one JSON object per line, ordered by id. pm init --import FILE in another clone reads it, to move a project's items into a new repo's store.

options:
  -h, --help   show this help message and exit
  --store DIR  the work store at DIR (<main checkout>/.pm/store/work), read through the pm service of the clone DIR belongs to, without the repo's config
===== pm version --help
usage: pm version [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm prime --help
usage: pm prime [-h] [--rules N | --state | --request-rule | --subagent]
                [--hook-json]

options:
  -h, --help      show this help message and exit
  --rules N       only chunk N of the rules and the command list, under a title naming its sections: one hook each on SessionStart and SubagentStart, since Claude Code passes a hook's text inline only up to 10,000 characters
  --state         only pm init, pm where and pm show, cut at a line to 10,000 characters: the last SessionStart hook; in a Codex session it opens with a line naming the pm hooks Codex skips, untrusted or changed since trusted (codex app-server, asked again only when .codex/hooks.json or your Codex trust changed)
  --request-rule  only the rule for asking the owner in the session's mode (autopilot, or interactive: pm interactive --help), under a heading naming it, read from the hook input's session_id (run by hand, this session's), autopilot's when the mode cannot be read: its own SessionStart hook, beside the rules chunks, never on SubagentStart
  --subagent      only pm's git rule for agents, one line: the last SubagentStart hook, beside the rules chunks
  --hook-json     read the SessionStart or SubagentStart input on stdin and print the hook's JSON envelope, as Claude Code and Codex read it
===== pm hook --help
usage: pm hook [-h] {stop,owner-request,ask,session-end} ...

positional arguments:
  {stop,owner-request,ask,session-end}
    stop                Stop: block once while records are uncommitted: in a worktree, any in its own records workspace (each named, conflict markers too); in the main checkout, those in the store that this session's tool calls name, leaving out a record that only a still-running subagent's prompt names
    owner-request       Stop: block once while the reply asks the owner for something no open need or action of this session covers
    ask                 PermissionRequest and PostToolUse of AskUserQuestion (Claude Code), PostToolUse of request_user_input_async and UserPromptSubmit (Codex): raise the session's ask-tool question as a question need the owner can answer on the site too, and close it with the first answer
    session-end         SessionEnd: remove the ended session's interactive-mode file (pm interactive --help)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
===== pm hook stop --help
usage: pm hook stop [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm hook owner-request --help
usage: pm hook owner-request [-h] [--runtime {claude,codex}]

Stop: block once while the reply asks the owner for something no open need or action of this session covers. In a session in interactive mode (pm interactive --help) it lets every stop through without the judge. A small model on the session's own runtime judges the reply: Claude Haiku through claude -p for Claude Code, gpt-5.6-luna through codex exec for Codex, each run from an empty temp directory with no hooks, no project instructions and no saved session. Each runtime's hook entry names its runtime; pm never guesses it. When the check cannot run (the runtime's CLI is missing, fails or times out), it exits 1 with the cause on stderr: Claude Code shows it, Codex lets the stop through.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --runtime {claude,codex}
                        the session's runtime, which the judge runs on; pm init writes claude in .claude/settings.json and codex in .codex/hooks.json (default: claude, as an entry written before this flag)
===== pm hook ask --help
usage: pm hook ask [-h] --runtime {claude,codex}

Raise the session's ask-tool question as a question need under the newest open task the session holds (else none), so the owner can answer it on the site as well as in the session; the first answer to close the need wins, and no transcript or rollout is read. Claude Code runs it on PermissionRequest of AskUserQuestion, beside the question's dialog, for up to a day: it raises the question and waits on one call to the pm service; on a site reply it closes the need as answered on the site and answers the tool with the reply, one "<question>: <answer>" line per question (an answer naming an option's label is that option; a reply with no such line answers every question whole); on a close elsewhere it exits; when Claude Code stops it (SIGTERM, on Esc or the session ending) it closes the need as dismissed. Claude Code runs it on PostToolUse of AskUserQuestion too: it closes the call's open question with the dialog's answers. A subagent's call steps aside. Codex runs it on PostToolUse of request_user_input_async, which returns at once: it raises the question once, by its call id (tool_use_id), and steps aside for any other tool; and on UserPromptSubmit: it closes the session's open questions with the prompt the owner typed, leaving out a message the pm service relayed. The pm service closes a Codex question as answered on the site before it queues the site's answer into the session. It fails open: on its own error it says why on stderr, prints nothing and exits 0.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --runtime {claude,codex}
                        the session's runtime; pm init writes claude in .claude/settings.json and codex in .codex/hooks.json
===== pm hook session-end --help
usage: pm hook session-end [-h]

options:
  -h, --help  show this help message and exit
===== pm commit --help
usage: pm commit [-h] -m MESSAGE [--sprint ID] [PATH ...]

Commit only the named records, so other sessions' uncommitted edits are left alone. With no path, it lists what is uncommitted and commits nothing. In a worktree, records/ is the worktree's own records workspace (branch drafts/<worktree>, made by pm init): pm commit first brings it onto the records branch (follow: the records branch's changes since its base merged into your uncommitted edits with git merge-file, conflict markers where both changed the same lines), refuses a named file with conflict markers, naming the records commits that changed it under your edit, and refuses a file the shared store has uncommitted edits to; then it commits the named files on the records branch in one commit, so other sessions see them. A design page goes into the workspace's draft commit instead, tagged with its sprint: the open sprint whose Design pages link the page and in which this session holds a task (--sprint names one of several; none is refused: link the page first). It is published when that sprint closes (pm sprint close), once its code is on main; until then the site shows it as this worktree's version of the page. A page committed back to its published text leaves the draft. A page's draft in a workspace belongs to one sprint: a page drafted there for another open sprint is refused. A follow that leaves a drafted page with conflict markers keeps its sprint, and that sprint's close refuses until it is resolved and committed again (or discarded). Every other pm write from a worktree commits on the records branch, then follows the workspace. In the main checkout, records/ is the shared store and a design page is refused: edit design pages in a worktree. A record uses only these fenced blocks: ::: decision, ::: result and ```mermaid; put a one-line reading under each diagram or large table. An image file (.svg, .png, .jpg, .webp) beside a record is named and committed like one; the site serves it next to the record's page.

positional arguments:
  PATH                  a record you edited: records/<…>.md, a path in the workspace (in the main checkout, the store), relative to it from inside it, or relative to it from anywhere (sprints/<…>.md)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -m, --message MESSAGE
                        what the hand edit changed
  --sprint ID           the sprint a named design page's draft belongs to, when several open sprints in which this session holds a task link it
